you guys wanted to tell the story of  working at a help desk and what it was  like it was an i.t help desk for school  mainly computer issues so  yeah they ended up being one of the most  defining  jobs i had mostly because it was so  miserable but looked good from the  outside  so i always had a strong interest in  computers got to know some people at  this  university doing contract work repairing  computers  and eventually a position opened up  for another spot that was a contract  position paid a little more  sounded a little better so i went for it  got it  eventually kind of moved around in the  ranks a little bit but there ended up  being a  full-time non-contract position open for  someone at the help desk and i was told  i should go for it  it sounded pretty nice it had benefits  it was better pay it was  more stable because it wasn't contract  so i went for it and i got the job  and i spent about two and a half years  there it was during this time  which i might get to a little later that  i decided i was going to  move towards being a programmer  so the day-to-day was  pretty miserable it was mostly just  answering phones answering tickets  answering emails and it was pretty much  always the same thing every day it was  mostly i forgot my password how do you  reset it  and it's like you you click the link and  you fill out the thing  and uh that was that was like the  majority of  the things to handle was just that over  and over and over  so after a while we kind of had a script  and we would just be like hey try this  do this this doesn't work do this and  eventually you know if they couldn't get  it at all they'd have to send an id  and prove that they are who they are and  then we could reset it  as you know the it behind the scenes  deal  however there were a lot of other things  that came in and a lot of other  interesting scenarios  we got calls for and stuff like that so  i started to kind of see  a more broad view of how everything  intertwined because  i was like i felt like i was the  connector i would like  connect whoever was having a problem to  the right team that could fix it  and you know after doing that for a  while you get really good at it  you're really good at figuring out who  does what in  a rather larger organization  so it was kind of defining for me i got  to kind of play around with  you know seeing where things might land  basically but also just going in and  doing that for eight to ten hours a day  was not exactly fun after a while there  were moments you know there were moments  with co-workers that were great there  were some calls that were great there's  some people you'd run into that were  great  but the majority of them were just kind  of monotone boring  things just kind of doing the same thing  repeating the same pattern  and getting interrupted by phone calls  all the time which is kind of a pet  peeve of mine i just don't like being  interrupted by phone calls to begin with  so  probably not the greatest fit for me  because of that but i  kind of learned to deal with it and i  think i grew his person became a better  person for it  if you get a chance to work at a help  desk  i think that you should do it but don't  plan on being there too long  i i feel like it's it is one of those  jobs that kind of  after a while kind of sucks the soul out  of you just the work is so repetitive  and  uh you run into a lot of people that  will like  numb your mind with the stuff they see  that's so uh  or the stuff they say that's so obvious  like how do i represent my password and  it's just like  you click the i forgot my password  button for the millionth time  like why why do you call me for this  so there's a lot of that and it's that's  just one example but there's like that  for a lot of things there's  stuff like how do i turn off my computer  it's like you  go to shut down turn it off you know  start button oh how do i do that you  click the  start button probably at the bottom left  and then you know like little basic walk  through stuff like that  which is kind of a bit crazy to have to  say to people after a while it feels  like  yeah but there are some good ones there  are there were a lot of legitimate  problems too and those are really  interesting i i actually enjoyed the  legitimate problems the ones that really  bugged me were the ones that were like  this isn't even a problem you just have  no idea what you're doing  uh i guess i guess it's a valid reason  to call the help desk but  i just felt like i could be more useful  than walking people through really  basic obvious stuff and that kind of  annoyed me after a while  which you know i guess sounds kind of uh  doesn't sound so great to say now feels  like you know i'm  i don't know something about that  doesn't sit right to say but it was kind  of the truth it was like an underlying  feeling and i i couldn't really help  that i was feeling that  you know because that's that's just the  way it is you feel what you feel  you can control how you express it but  you can't really  say you didn't feel it if you did so  i'm just taking a step back was  interesting it's definitely  definitely a good growing experience to  do something like that  it really tests your patience and also  teaches you a lot of patience because  you can't just like blow up on people  you know you have some days where you  get the same call over and over you get  really annoying calls or you start  getting frustrated or you can't get a  break because  no one else is there and the calls are  queued up all the way with like 10  people waiting and it's just like i'm  one guy  and i and you have to like log  everything in between calls so  while you're logging the previous one  the next one's calling and it's just  like holy crap can i get a moment to  myself to think  you know that sort of thing so you learn  to deal with that and to deal with that  a lot of times you just  you know you just kind of put people on  hold for a minute sometimes if you have  to because  things are piling up you can't scatter  yourself too wide you just got to be  like okay hold for a minute let me do  this  okay and continue so a lot of  managed time management became really  really important and just kind of  figuring out how to be polite with  people while you do that  so that's not a skill everybody has you  know especially more  technical people don't always have that  skill because they just spend so much  time  technical stuff i think and less on the  social aspect so  yeah yeah definitely uh definitely worth  doing  but not something you could do your  whole life in my opinion at least not  without  driving yourself crazy everyone else  that was doing that for longer than me  seemed like it was driving them crazy  they still continue to do it which  i didn't quite get but i did to some  extent like it's  it's in some places it's hard to find  something  better but usually you learn so much  that you can  find something better pretty quickly so  that's kind of what happened to me  it was also the time period was like  okay i need to go back to school i need  to  self-improve so i can do something else  i don't want to do this my whole life  so that's my quick and dirty version of  my helpdesk story  let me know if you have any questions  down below and  thanks for watching peace out
 

